Now I understand the GREIS packet format. The packet is: `<id0><id1><len_hex:2><payload><checksum>`. The fuzzer's `build_greis_packet` builds: `<id0><id1><payload><checksum>\r\n`. But the actual GREIS format expects the length in hex after the ID. Let me re-examine the fuzzer's `build_greis_packet`:

```c
static size_t build_greis_packet(const uint8_t *data, size_t len, uint8_t *out)
{
    if (len < 2) return 0;
    if (len > 200) len = 200;

    // Standard GREIS message IDs
    static const char *greis_ids[] = {"RE", "ER", "PM", "RC", "RD", "SI", "EL"};
    ...
    size_t id_idx = data[0] % num_ids;
    out[0] = greis_ids[id_idx][0];
    out[1] = greis_ids[id_idx][1];

    size_t payload_len = len - 2;
    if (payload_len > 0) {
        memcpy(out + 2, data + 2, payload_len);
    }

    // Simple XOR checksum for GREIS
    uint8_t cs = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 2 + payload_len; i++) {
        cs ^= out[i];
    }
    out[2 + payload_len] = cs;
    out[3 + payload_len] = '\r';
    out[4 + payload_len] = '\n';

    return 5 + payload_len;
}
```

Wait, this doesn't match the actual GREIS format. The actual format is `<id0><id1><len_hex:2><payload><checksum>`. The fuzzer's format is `<id0><id1><payload><checksum>\r\n`. This means the fuzzer's GREIS packets might not be parsed correctly by the lexer.

Hmm, but the fuzzer is what OSS-Fuzz uses, so it must work somehow. Let me look at the GREIS lexer state machine more carefully. Let me look at the beginning of the GREIS parsing.

